Hi,

This mail goes to lots of people; the announcement list and sysops at sunsite
and tsx-11.


For the announces:
------------------

KISS: I've uploaded kiss-0.00.tar.gz and kiss.lsm to
sunsite.unc.edu:/pub/Linux/Incoming and to tsx-11.mit.edu:/Incoming. Kiss is
my "keep it simple shell" which is expecially designed for rescue missions. It
has lots of programs on-board: from "ls" to "umount". It is also used on the
SAR (Search-And-Rescue) disk maker. I modelled Kiss after David I. Bell's
"sash" (Stand-Alone Shell). The idea is the same: have a shell that has
on-board utilities. But Kiss also features redirection, piping, background
process starting etcetera. In functionality, Kiss is somewhere between Sash
and Tcsh. Though not as sophisticated.

SAR: I've uploaded SAR-2.20.tar.gz and SAR.lsm to sunsite and tsx, also to the
incoming directories. SAR is a shell script with utilities which creates for
you a bootable rescue disk; which you can use when your hard disk crashes.
Hope that you'll have your backup ready by then. SAR is improved: (a) it
copies files to the rescue disk with my "holecp" utility, which means that
holes in files are preserved. I'm told that in the future "cp" might be
re-written to support holes too, but in the mean time SAR copies files with
"holecp". Furthermore (b), SAR now uses Kiss as the login-shell interpreter,
instead of the former Sash.I think that you'll find this a major improvement.

If you're planning on retrieving SAR, you don't have to get the Kiss archive
too. Binaries of Kiss are in the SAR archive. Get Kiss only if you want to
look at the sources, or play around with it. Or of course if you have useful
extensions / bug fixes.

PRIMARY SITE:  Both for Kiss and for SAR, the primary site is ftp.icce.rug.nl,
directory /pub/unix. I'm just uploading to sunsite and tsx once in a while,
so.. if changes are to be expected, check on ftp.icce.rug.nl first. Since
Kiss is version 0.00, I expect some bug reports there.. though I've tested it,
and it works.


LSM Entries
-----------

I'm including the LSM entries here, for reference:

